package diag

// Package diag defines compiler diagnostics and renders them for people.
// It is a port of the Go compiler's package diag, function by function.
//
// Every stage of the pipeline (loader, lexer, parser, resolver, type
// checker, code generation backend) reports problems in user input as a
// list of Diagnostic rather than by stopping. A Diagnostic carries a
// Severity, a message, a Span into the source, and optionally related
// notes, such as one pointing at an earlier declaration a duplicate
// collides with.
//
// A Severity is error, warning or note. An error stops compilation: a
// program with one is never generated, built or dumped. A warning reports
// something suspicious that stops nothing, and is printed as one; a
// program with only warnings compiles as one with none does. A note only
// adds to another diagnostic, among its related. error_count and
// has_errors count the errors of a list, which is what every stage's and
// the driver's decisions count, warnings aside. A warning about code never
// used sets unused, its span the code an editor may dim; nothing rendered
// shows it.
//
// A related note is a Related, not a Diagnostic as in Go: Tack rejects a
// type that holds itself, even through a list, so a note cannot have notes
// of its own. No Go diagnostic's notes have notes either, so nothing
// rendered differs.
//
// render and render_all produce one compact line per diagnostic, which
// the Go compiler's golden tests pin. render_pretty and render_all_pretty add the offending
// source line with a marker under the span, for the command line: a caret
// under its first byte and a tilde under each of the rest, with nothing
// after. A source line is shown without its newline, \n or \r\n, so one
// saved with CRLF line endings looks the same as one saved with LF. Each
// appends to a list[uint8], where Go's returns a string, and returns the
// builtin append's error when the list cannot grow, having appended some
// of the text then. Each takes a source.Set, which finds the file each
// span points into by the source.FileID the span carries, so a diagnostic
// and its notes may be in different files of one package; a file
// compiled on its own is the one file of its Set. Every file must already
// have its line starts recorded by the lexer. A span in no file of the Set
// is a compiler bug, and is rendered with <unknown file> in place of a
// position, as Go renders it.
